---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
import WangcaiSeries from '../../components/WangcaiSeries.astro';
const P = 'https://media.huanyuchen.com/misc/joshua-tree/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Four Visits to the Same Desert — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Joshua Tree, four times between 2022 and 2025: a 3 a.m. lookout, a boulder field under storm clouds, Orion over the Joshua trees, and a field of giant hands with Wangcai.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2025</p>
  <h1>Four Visits to the Same Desert</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Joshua Tree, again and again: a 3 a.m. lookout, a boulder field, Orion over the yuccas and a field of giant hands.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Four visits · September 2022 – July 2025</strong><br />
    Keys View at 3 a.m. (Sep 2022) · Jumbo Rocks under storm clouds (Oct 2022) ·
    a night of stars, then Joshua trees by day, the Cholla Cactus Garden and Pinto Basin Road (Dec 2024) ·
    giant hands at sunset and stars over Twentynine Palms, with Wangcai (Jul 2025)<br />
    three of the four visits photographed mostly after dark · every season but spring
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.webp'} alt="Map of the Joshua Tree area showing where photos were taken on four visits: Keys View, Jumbo Rocks, the town of Joshua Tree, the Cholla Cactus Garden, the giant hand sculptures off Coyote Valley Road, and Twentynine Palms." /></a>
    <figcaption>Four visits on one map. Each stop is placed where its photo was taken.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Some places you visit once. Joshua Tree, for us, became a place we keep returning to, four times in three years
    by the camera roll's count, in September, October, December and July. The honest reason is not the park itself
    but the places to stay. The towns strung along Highway 62 at the park's northern edge, Yucca Valley, Joshua Tree
    and Twentynine Palms, are full of good, affordable homestays, and that turns the desert into an easy weekend.
    You book a house, drive out, and the park is a few minutes down the road.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the photos show we did with those weekends is mostly look up. Joshua Tree National Park is an International
    Dark Sky Park, and on three of the four visits most of the pictures were taken after dark: at a mountain lookout at
    three in the morning, in a garden at one in the morning, and in a desert backyard just before midnight. Below are
    the four visits in order, each reconstructed from its photos' times and places.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sep-2022">September 2022: Keys View at 3 a.m.</h2>
  <p>
    The first visit is the strangest, and there was no particular reason for it. The night before, we suddenly felt
    like driving out to the desert, and so we did: easy come, easy go. There are no daytime photos of it at all. The
    camera roll picks up at 2:58 on a Wednesday morning, September 28, at <strong>Keys View</strong>, the overlook at the end of a dead-end road on the
    crest of the Little San Bernardino Mountains, about 5,185 feet up. By day it is the park's most famous view: the
    whole Coachella Valley spread out below, the San Andreas Fault running along its floor, and on a clear day the
    Salton Sea shining far to the south. At three in the morning it is something else. The valley is a river of
    orange light, Palm Springs and its neighbors glowing under a dome of their own haze, and across it the dark bulk of
    Mount San Jacinto stands against the glow.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'keys-view-lights.webp'} alt="Night view from a dark mountain ridge down into a valley full of orange city lights, with a large mountain silhouetted against the glow and a few stars above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Keys View, 2:58 a.m.: the Coachella Valley's lights, with Mount San Jacinto beyond.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then the camera turned upward and stayed there for half an hour. Between 2:58 and 3:34 there are some twenty
    long exposures of the sky, and between them, screenshots of a star-chart app, the kind you hold up to the sky to
    have it name what you're looking at. The app's labels tell you what was overhead that autumn night: Taurus and
    Cancer among them, and a small red dot marked Mars. (In the autumn of 2022 Mars was indeed in Taurus, brightening
    toward its December opposition.) Phone exposures of a dark sky are mostly black, but the best of them, lightly
    brightened, show the stars as they were: sharp, many, and with nothing between them and the lookout.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'keys-view-stars.webp'} alt="A dark star field photographed from Keys View, with scattered bright stars and a small tight cluster at upper right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sky over Keys View at 3:03 a.m., lightly brightened.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="oct-2022">October 2022: Jumbo Rocks under the clouds</h2>
  <p>
    Two and a half weeks later we were back, this time for a weekend in a homestay in Twentynine Palms. The drive out on Friday,
    October 14, passed Cabazon, where Interstate 10 squeezes through the San Gorgonio Pass between the two highest
    mountains in Southern California. At 5:56 p.m. we stopped at the outlet malls there. Over the rooftops, the north
    face of Mount San Jacinto caught the evening light. It rises more than 10,000 feet in a few miles, one of the
    steepest mountain faces in the country, and from the parking lot it looks close enough to touch.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cabazon-san-jacinto.webp'} alt="Mount San Jacinto's steep face in evening light, rising beyond the tiled rooftops of an outlet mall at Cabazon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mount San Jacinto from Cabazon, on the way out, October 14.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next afternoon, just before two, we were in the middle of the park at <strong>Jumbo Rocks</strong>. If you
    picture Joshua Tree as piles of giant rounded boulders, this is where the picture comes from. The rock is
    monzogranite that cooled underground millions of years ago, cracked into blocks along joints, and weathered from
    the cracks inward while still buried; when the soil around it eroded away, it left these stacks of rounded,
    house-sized stones. A campground threads between them, with tents tucked into the gaps.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jumbo-rocks-clouds.webp'} alt="Heavy gray and white clouds with patches of blue sky over a long ridge of rounded granite boulders." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Storm clouds over Jumbo Rocks.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jumbo-rocks.webp'} alt="Rounded tan granite boulders and yuccas at Jumbo Rocks under a deep blue sky with towering white clouds on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same minute, looking the other way.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That October afternoon the sky was doing more than the rocks. Big weather was building: in one direction a ceiling of heavy gray cloud with blue tearing
    through it, in the other, towering white cumulus stacked along the horizon over the boulder piles. The three photos
    from Jumbo Rocks were all taken within thirty seconds of each other, which is about how long it takes to turn
    around on top of a rock and decide the whole view is worth having.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dec-2024">December 2024: Orion over the Joshua trees</h2>
  <p>
    The third visit came two years later, in December, with a house in the town of Joshua Tree as base. We drove
    in on the afternoon of December 8, as the sky over the desert turned pink.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dusk-dec-2024.webp'} alt="Open high-desert scrub with cholla and a few houses at dusk, low mountains on the horizon and pink and blue streaks of cloud across the sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Arriving at dusk, 4:39 p.m., December 8.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    This is the visit with the best night sky. Between 12:44 and 1:33 on the morning of December 9, the camera roll fills with
    stars, and halfway through the garden's Joshua trees appear, lit from below. The result is
    the best photo of the whole series: a single Joshua tree glowing gold against a deep blue sky, with
    <strong>Orion</strong> standing directly above it, belt and sword and all. In December, Orion is the winter sky's
    centerpiece, high in the south after midnight.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'joshua-tree-orion.webp'} alt="A Joshua tree lit golden from below against a dark blue night sky, with the constellation Orion clearly visible above it and dark mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Joshua tree and Orion, 1:22 a.m., December 9.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The Joshua tree is not really a tree. It is a yucca, <em>Yucca brevifolia</em>, a giant relative of the agave,
    and it grows almost nowhere outside the Mojave Desert. The usual story is that Mormon settlers crossing the
    desert in the nineteenth century named it after the biblical Joshua, its branches raised like arms in prayer,
    pointing the way west. Lit up at night, it looks less like a prophet and more like a firework frozen mid-burst.
    Next to it in the garden, a tall columnar cactus got the same treatment.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cactus-night.webp'} alt="A tall green columnar cactus lit from below against a starry sky, with a lit Joshua tree at the edge of the frame." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A columnar cactus under the stars, 1:23 a.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'desert-stars-2024.webp'} alt="A wide star-filled sky above a dark mountain ridge, with two Joshua trees faintly lit at the bottom." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The full sky over the ridge, 1:29 a.m.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Pointed straight up without a foreground, the phone found more stars than the eye expects: Orion again, upper
    right, and hundreds of fainter stars scattered across the frame above the ridge. That night's star-chart
    screenshot labels Mars and Jupiter among them.
  </p>

  <h3>The Cholla Cactus Garden and Pinto Basin Road</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-dec-2024.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-dec-2024.webp'} alt="Map of December 9, 2024: from the town of Joshua Tree through the park to the Cholla Cactus Garden and Pinto Basin Road, then south to Mecca and Route 86." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>December 9: through the park and down to the Coachella Valley.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We drove in through the West Entrance just before noon, past the boulder piles and Joshua trees along Park
    Boulevard, and kept going south.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'park-boulders.webp'} alt="A long hill of rounded tan granite boulders with Joshua trees and yuccas in front, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boulders and Joshua trees along Park Boulevard, 11:58 a.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'park-joshua-trees.webp'} alt="A wide flat of Joshua trees and dry grass with a pile of boulders and a mountain range behind, under a blue sky with wispy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Joshua trees by day, 11:59 a.m.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By 1:12 that afternoon we were deep in the park at the <strong>Cholla Cactus Garden</strong>, on Pinto Basin Road.
    This is where the park changes deserts. North and west of here is the higher, cooler Mojave, the land of Joshua
    trees; south and east the land drops into the lower, hotter Colorado Desert, a part of the Sonoran. Right on the
    seam grows a dense stand of teddy-bear cholla, <em>Cylindropuntia bigelovii</em>. From a distance they look soft
    and fuzzy, which is the joke in the name. Up close every stem is armored in barbed silver spines, and the
    segments break off at a touch. That is why it is also called jumping cholla.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cholla-garden.webp'} alt="A field of pale teddy-bear cholla cacti on a gravel flat under a bright winter sun, with a mountain range behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Cholla Cactus Garden, 1:12 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cholla-garden-2.webp'} alt="Cholla cacti and desert shrubs backlit by a low sun, with a long line of mountains across the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Backlit cholla, looking toward the Pinto Mountains.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The low December sun was almost straight into the camera, and backlighting is what cholla are made for: every
    spine catches the light and each cactus gets a glowing outline. Eight minutes later we were driving south on
    <strong>Pinto Basin Road</strong>, which runs on through the empty Colorado Desert half of the park toward
    the Cottonwood entrance and Interstate 10. It is a long, straight two-lane road with creosote on both sides and
    hardly another car.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pinto-basin-road.webp'} alt="An empty two-lane road running straight across flat desert scrub toward the horizon, under a deep blue sky with wispy clouds, seen through a windshield." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pinto Basin Road, 1:20 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Out the bottom of the park, the road drops into the Coachella Valley, and the desert turns into farmland. At
    2:13 we were in <strong>Mecca</strong>, at the valley's southern end near the Salton Sea, where the fields are
    date palms. The Coachella Valley grows the great majority of America's dates, from palms first brought from
    North Africa and the Middle East a little over a century ago. Four minutes later, on <strong>Route 86</strong>,
    a long freight train of double-stacked containers slid past below the Santa Rosa Mountains.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mecca-date-palm.webp'} alt="A date palm by a sandy lot behind a wire fence, with rows of date palms and a mountain beyond, seen through a car window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Date palms in Mecca, 2:13 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'route-86-train.webp'} alt="A freight train of orange double-stacked containers crossing flat desert in front of hazy blue mountain ranges." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A container train below the Santa Rosa Mountains, from Route 86.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="jul-2025">July 2025: giant hands, with Wangcai</h2>
  <p>
    The fourth visit was over the Independence Day holiday in 2025, and this time Wangcai, our cream-colored dog,
    came along. (She joined the family in January 2025, and has
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">her own page</a>.) On the evening of July 5, just after sunset, we were north of
    Highway 62 on <strong>Coyote Valley Road</strong>, in the open high desert between the towns of Joshua Tree and
    Twentynine Palms. The desert there is a wide basin of creosote bushes running out to a wall of bare mountains,
    and a waxing moon was already up in a sky fading from blue to pink.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'desert-moon.webp'} alt="A wide high-desert basin of creosote bushes at dusk, with a long mountain range on the horizon, a pink band low in the sky and the moon above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coyote Valley at dusk, with the moon up, 8:11 p.m., July 5.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then, rising out of the creosote, the hands. This is <strong><em>Transmission</em></strong>, an installation by
    the South African artist <strong>Daniel Popper</strong>, who is known for monumental figurative sculpture. There are four of them, each two or three times a person's height,
    white and raised palm-out like a greeting or a vow, and in the middle of the palm of each one is a round window
    ringed with mirrored mosaic that catches the last light. Between them is a giant face, eyes closed, a crescent
    moon cut through its forehead, resting its chin on two long arms dotted with holes and inlaid with colored glass.
    A path leads up to them through the scrub, and other people were walking it in the dusk.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hands-approach.webp'} alt="Four giant white hand sculptures and a large face sculpture rising above desert scrub at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first sight of the hands, 8:12 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hands-field.webp'} alt="A sandy path leading to a ring of stones in front of four giant white hands and a face sculpture, at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The path in, 8:16 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Close up, the central figure is the one that stays with you. The face is serene, almost sleeping, its cheek opened
    up into a mosaic of amber and brown glass, the hands on either side holding up their mirrored eyes. A small circle
    of stones lies on the ground in front of it, like an altar or a campfire ring. Popper's piece joins a long
    high-desert habit of big outdoor art on open land; Noah Purifoy's outdoor museum of assemblage sculpture is a few miles away, in the town of Joshua Tree.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hands-face.webp'} alt="Close view of a giant serene face sculpture with closed eyes and a crescent-moon cutout, resting on two arms, flanked by two giant raised hands with mosaic-ringed holes in their palms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The face between the hands.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Wangcai, for her part, had her back to the art. Between 8:13 and 8:14 she sat for a series
    of portraits on the sand beside the wire fence, the hands lined up behind her on the ridge, tongue out in the warm
    evening air. On a July evening the desert floor gives back the day's heat long after the sun is gone, and she
    did her posing with her mouth open. She is cream-colored, almost white in some lights, and with the pale hands
    behind her it is hard to say which one the photos are of.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-hands-sit.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog in a dark harness sitting on sand, looking to the side with her tongue out, with the giant hand sculptures behind a wire fence." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai and the hands, 8:14 p.m.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-hands-smile.webp'} alt="The cream-colored dog sitting upright on the sand facing the camera, with the hand sculptures in the distance under a blue dusk sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sitting for her portrait.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the close-up her ears are up, her eyes
    are amber, and she is looking off to the side, with the hands blurred into
    the background over her shoulder. By 8:16 the light was going, and we left the hands to the dusk.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wangcai-closeup.webp'} alt="Close-up of the cream-colored dog with amber eyes and tongue out, with the hand sculptures blurred behind a fence." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, looking elsewhere.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That night, back at our base in Twentynine Palms, the camera went up one last time. Between 10:52 and 11:01 p.m. there are
    ten frames of the sky, and in the best of them the stars come through thick and small, with a faint, soft glow
    across the middle of the frame. It is the fourth visit and the third night spent looking up from this desert.
    The record doesn't explain the habit, but it does show it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'twentynine-palms-stars.webp'} alt="A deep blue night sky full of small stars with a faint soft glow through the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Stars over Twentynine Palms, 10:58 p.m., July 5.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Those night skies, and the constellations hiding in them, probably deserve a post of their own someday.
  </p>

  <p class="muted"><small>Maps built from the photos' timestamps and locations. Photo locations have been removed
    from the published images. Maps: map data © OpenStreetMap contributors; each stop is placed where its photo was
    taken (towns are marked at their centers), places we stayed are never marked, and the dashed lines show the order of
    stops, not the exact roads.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
  <WangcaiSeries current="/misc/joshua-tree" />
